| r | w1 | w2 | w3 | w4 | \|x - sum w n\| |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 1e-09 | 0.263 | 0.263 | 0.316 | 0.158 | 0.000 |
| 1e-03 | 0.263 | 0.263 | 0.316 | 0.158 | 0.000 |
| 1e-01 | 0.261 | 0.261 | 0.307 | 0.170 | 0.032 |
| 1e+00 | 0.255 | 0.255 | 0.277 | 0.213 | 0.149 |
| 1e+01 | 0.251 | 0.251 | 0.254 | 0.244 | 0.234 |
| 1e+03 | 0.250 | 0.250 | 0.250 | 0.250 | 0.250 |

\image(figures/lle_weights.svg)
// result_end

\caption(Left: the four neighbours of \(x\) and their weights as \(r\to0\), the minimum-norm exact reconstruction \((5,5,6,3)/19\). Right: the weights as the ridge grows; all tend to \(1/k=0.25\) (grey). Table: the weights and the reconstruction error \(\lVert x-\sum_jw_jn_j\rVert\) for six values of \(r\).)
\alttext(Left: a black point x at the origin joined by grey lines to four blue neighbours at 1 0, minus 1 0, 0 1 and 0 minus 2, labelled w1 equals 0.263, w2 equals 0.263, w3 equals 0.316 and w4 equals 0.158. Right: three curves of weight against log10 r from minus 6 to 3. They are flat at 0.316, 0.263 and 0.158 up to about r equals 0.01, then converge to 0.25 by r equals 100.)

The library reproduces \((5,5,6,3)/19\). Up to \(r=10^{-3}\) the weights and the exact
reconstruction are unchanged to three decimals. By \(r=1\) the weights have moved halfway to equal,
and the reconstruction misses \(x\) by 0.149. At \(r=1000\) the reconstruction is the neighbours'
mean, \((0,-0.25)\), at distance 0.25.

>> Seven points on a line

Take seven equally spaced points \(x_i=(1+0.6i,\,2+0.8i)\), \(i=0,\dots,6\), on a line in the plane, with
\(k=2\). An interior point is the midpoint of its two neighbours, so its weights are
\((\tfrac12,\tfrac12)\). An end point, \(x_0\), has neighbours \(x_1,x_2\) on the same side and is the
extrapolation \(x_0=2x_1-x_2\), so its weights are \((2,-1)\). Both reconstructions are exact and
are also exact for any affine function of the position on the line, in particular for the
index \(i\). So \(M\) has a two-dimensional null space, spanned by \(\mathbf 1\) and \(i\). The
embedding is the centred and normalised index, \(\pm(i-3)/\sqrt{28}\) (since \(\sum(i-3)^2=28\)). The
sign is arbitrary; datamunge makes the largest entry positive, which on this symmetric
example is the first point's.

>> Points on a spiral

The next example is curved. Sixty points on the spiral \((t\cos t,\ t\sin t)\), \(t\in[1.5\pi,4.5\pi]\),
evenly spaced in \(t\), are the Swiss roll seen end on. The arc length
\(s(t)=\int_0^t\sqrt{1+\tau^2}\,d\tau=\tfrac12(t\sqrt{1+t^2}+\operatorname{asinh}t)\) is the manifold's own
coordinate. Successive points are 1.5 apart on the inside and 4.5 apart on the outside, so even
spacing in \(t\) is far from even in \(s\). With \(k=2=D\) the matrix \(C\) is invertible and no
reconstruction is exact. With \(k=4>D\) every point is reconstructed exactly, the situation
of *Why the ridge is not optional*.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/lle_spiral.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

using DF = dm::dstruct::DataFrame;

int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  // Seven equally spaced points on a line in the plane.
  DF line;
  line.add_column("x", (1.0 + 0.6 * dm::seql(0.0, 6.0, 7)).values());
  line.add_column("y", (2.0 + 0.8 * dm::seql(0.0, 6.0, 7)).values());
  const dm::stats::LLE on_line(line, {"x", "y"}, {.n_components = 1, .n_neighbors = 2, .regularization = 1e-9});
  std::vector<std::string> nbrs, wts;
  std::vector<double> theory;
  for (int i = 0; i < 7; ++i) {
    nbrs.push_back(std::format("{}", on_line.neighbors()[i]));
    wts.push_back(std::format("{::.3f}", on_line.weights()[i]));
    theory.push_back((3 - i) / std::sqrt(28.0));
  }
  DF table;
  table.add_column("i", dm::seql(0.0, 6.0, 7).values());
  table.add_column("neighbours", nbrs);
  table.add_column("weights", wts);
  table.add_column("Y", on_line.dimension(0));
  table.add_column("(3 - i)/sqrt(28)", theory);
  std::print("{}\n\nEigenvalues of M, smallest first: {:.1e}, {:.1e}, then {:.4f}.\n\n", table.to_md(),
             on_line.eigenvalues()[6], on_line.eigenvalues()[5], on_line.eigenvalues()[4]);

  // Sixty points on the spiral (t cos t, t sin t), evenly spaced in t.
  std::vector<double> sx, sz, s;
  for (int i = 0; i < 60; ++i) {
    const double t = 1.5 * std::numbers::pi * (1.0 + 2.0 * i / 59.0);
    sx.push_back(t * std::cos(t)), sz.push_back(t * std::sin(t));
    s.push_back(0.5 * (t * std::sqrt(1.0 + t * t) + std::asinh(t)));
  }
  DF spiral;
  spiral.add_column("x", sx);
  spiral.add_column("z", sz);
  spiral.add_column("s", s);
  DF fits;
  std::vector<double> ks{2, 4}, lam, cs, cx, cz, r2s, r2xz;
  std::vector<std::vector<double>> coords;
  for (const double k : ks) {
    const dm::stats::LLE lle(spiral, {"x", "z"}, {.n_components = 1, .n_neighbors = static_cast<std::size_t>(k)});
    lam.push_back(lle.eigenvalues()[58]);
    cs.push_back(lle.cost(s)), cx.push_back(lle.cost(sx)), cz.push_back(lle.cost(sz));
    DF fit = spiral;
    fit.add_column("Y", lle.dimension(0));
    r2s.push_back(dm::stats::LM(fit, "Y ~ s").r_squared());
    r2xz.push_back(dm::stats::LM(fit, "Y ~ x + z").r_squared());
    coords.push_back(lle.dimension(0));
  }
  fits.add_column("k", ks);
  fits.add_column("lambda_1", lam);
  fits.add_column("cost of s", cs);
  fits.add_column("cost of x", cx);
  fits.add_column("cost of z", cz);
  fits.add_column("R2 of Y on s", r2s);
  fits.add_column("R2 of Y on x, z", r2xz);
  DF::MarkdownOptions md;
  md.digits = 2;
  md.column_digits = {{"R2 of Y on s", 4}, {"R2 of Y on x, z", 4}};
  md.scientific = {"lambda_1", "cost of s", "cost of x", "cost of z"};
  std::print("{}\n", fits.to_md(md));

  const dm::stats::LLE chain(spiral, {"x", "z"}, {.n_components = 1, .n_neighbors = 2});
  auto view = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  for (std::size_t i = 0; i < 60; ++i)
    for (const auto j : chain.neighbors()[i])
      view.segments({sx[i]}, {sz[i]}, {sx[j]}, {sz[j]}, dm::RGB{148, 163, 184}, 1.2);
  view.points_colored(sx, sz, dm::graphics::value_colors(s), 5.0);
  view.x_limits(-12.0, 15.0).y_limits(-12.0, 15.0).title("Spiral and its 2-NN graph").x_label("x").y_label("z");

  auto embed = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  embed.points(s, coords[0], "k = 2", dm::RGB{37, 99, 235}, 5.0).points(s, coords[1], "k = 4", dm::RGB{220, 38, 38}, 5.0);
  embed.y_limits(-0.35, 0.65).title("LLE coordinate against arc length").x_label("arc length s").y_label("Y");

  auto fig = dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1, 2);
  fig.add(view).add(embed).size(1100, 480).fit_panels();
  fig.save_svg("build/lle_spiral.svg");
}
```
