// result_end

\caption(Left: sixty points on the spiral, coloured by arc length, with the edges of the 2-nearest-neighbour graph. Right: the one-dimensional LLE coordinate against arc length, for \(k=2\) (blue) and \(k=4\) (red). Tables: the seven-point line, with each point's neighbours, weights and embedding, against the closed form; then, for the spiral, the smallest non-trivial eigenvalue, the costs \(v^\top Mv\) of the centred unit-length arc length and raw coordinates, and how well the embedding is explained by \(s\) and by \((x,z)\).)
\alttext(Left: a spiral of sixty points, one and a half turns, coloured from purple at the inner end to yellow at the outer end, with grey edges joining successive points only. Right: blue points for k equals 2 lie on a straight rising line from about minus 0.18 at arc length 12 to 0.25 at arc length 102. Red points for k equals 4 form a U: they fall from 0.15 at arc length 12 to minus 0.16 near arc length 48, then rise to 0.29, so points on the two arms of the U share coordinates and the order along the spiral is lost.)

The line reproduces the closed form exactly: the weights are \((\tfrac12,\tfrac12)\) inside and
\((2,-1)\) at the ends, the two smallest eigenvalues are zero to rounding, and the embedding is
\((3-i)/\sqrt{28}\).

On the spiral with \(k=2\), the coordinate is a linear function of the *arc length*
(\(R^2=1.0000\)), not of \(t\), although the points are evenly spaced in \(t\). Near each point the
spiral is almost a straight segment. The weights place \(x_i\) between its neighbours in
proportion to the distances, and that is exactly what reconstructs \(s\). The cost table shows
the mechanism: \(s\) costs \(10^{-7}\), the raw coordinates \(x\) and \(z\) about \(2\times10^{-4}\), so the
cheapest coordinate is \(s\). With \(k=4\) the order flips. The four weights now reconstruct
every point exactly in the plane, so \(x\) and \(z\) become nearly free (they cost only what the
ridge allows), and \(s\), which the spiral's curvature stops from being reconstructed by a
four-point stencil, costs more. More than half of the embedding's variance is now a linear function of \(x\) and \(z\)
(\(R^2=0.54\)), and it folds back on itself, so it no longer orders the points along the spiral. The same competition decides the Swiss roll's
regularisation below.

> Checking the theory

>> A flat sheet is recovered exactly

If the data lie on a \(d\)-dimensional plane in \(\mathbb R^D\), every intrinsic coordinate is an
affine function of the raw coordinates. The argument of *Why the ridge is not optional* then
works in LLE's favour: as \(r\to0\) the weights reconstruct each point exactly, the plane's own
coordinates \((u,v)\) are null vectors of \(M\), and the embedding spans exactly
\(\{u-\bar u,\ v-\bar v\}\). The check: 500 points uniform on a \(10\times6\) rectangle, mapped into
\(\mathbb R^3\) by an affine map of rank 2, embedded with \(k=10\).

```{cpp, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

using DF = dm::dstruct::DataFrame;

int main() {
  // 500 points (u, v) on a 10 x 6 rectangle, placed in 3-D by an affine map of rank 2.
  dm::set_seed(5);
  const auto u = dm::runif(0.0, 10.0, 500), v = dm::runif(0.0, 6.0, 500);
  DF sheet;
  sheet.add_column("u", u.values());
  sheet.add_column("v", v.values());
  sheet.add_column("x", (0.8 * u - 0.3 * v + 1.0).values());
  sheet.add_column("y", (0.2 * u + 0.9 * v).values());
  sheet.add_column("z", (0.5 * u + 0.4 * v - 2.0).values());

  std::vector<double> regs{1e-9, 1e-3, 1e-1}, l1, l2, l3, r2u, r2v, orth;
  for (const double r : regs) {
    const dm::stats::LLE lle(sheet, {"x", "y", "z"}, {.n_components = 2, .n_neighbors = 10, .regularization = r});
    const auto& ev = lle.eigenvalues();
    l1.push_back(ev[498]), l2.push_back(ev[497]), l3.push_back(ev[496]);
    DF fit = sheet;
    fit.add_column("Y1", lle.dimension(0));
    fit.add_column("Y2", lle.dimension(1));
    r2u.push_back(dm::stats::LM(fit, "u ~ Y1 + Y2").r_squared());
    r2v.push_back(dm::stats::LM(fit, "v ~ Y1 + Y2").r_squared());
    const auto& Y = lle.embedding();
    double worst = 0.0;  // largest deviation of Y'Y from the identity, and of Y's column sums from 0
    for (std::size_t a = 0; a < 2; ++a) {
      double sum = 0.0;
      for (std::size_t i = 0; i < 500; ++i) sum += Y(i, a);
      worst = std::max(worst, std::abs(sum));
      for (std::size_t b = 0; b < 2; ++b) {
        double dot = 0.0;
        for (std::size_t i = 0; i < 500; ++i) dot += Y(i, a) * Y(i, b);
        worst = std::max(worst, std::abs(dot - (a == b ? 1.0 : 0.0)));
      }
    }
    orth.push_back(worst);
  }
  DF table;
  table.add_column("r", regs);
  table.add_column("lambda_1", l1);
  table.add_column("lambda_2", l2);
  table.add_column("lambda_3", l3);
  table.add_column("R2 of u", r2u);
  table.add_column("R2 of v", r2v);
  table.add_column("constraint error", orth);
  DF::MarkdownOptions md;
  md.digits = 2;
  md.column_digits = {{"R2 of u", 6}, {"R2 of v", 6}, {"r", 0}};
  md.scientific = {"r", "lambda_1", "lambda_2", "lambda_3", "constraint error"};
  std::print("{}\n", table.to_md(md));
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| r | lambda_1 | lambda_2 | lambda_3 | R2 of u | R2 of v | constraint error |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 1e-09 | -1.38e-15 | 3.24e-15 | 1.68e-06 | 1.000000 | 1.000000 | 5.40e-15 |
| 1e-03 | 1.79e-08 | 8.24e-08 | 3.15e-06 | 0.999084 | 0.999152 | 3.33e-15 |
| 1e-01 | 2.53e-05 | 9.38e-05 | 2.18e-04 | 0.982547 | 0.950397 | 1.80e-14 |
// result_end

\caption(LLE of a flat sheet in \(\mathbb R^3\) with \(k=10\), for three ridges: the three smallest non-trivial eigenvalues of \(M\), the \(R^2\) of the true coordinates \(u\) and \(v\) regressed on the embedding, and the largest error in the constraints \(Y^\top Y=I\), \(\mathbf 1^\top Y=0\).)

With \(r=10^{-9}\) the two embedding eigenvalues are zero to rounding, the third is \(10^9\) times
larger, and \(u\) and \(v\) are linear functions of the embedding to six decimals: LLE recovers the
sheet up to an affine map, as predicted. The default ridge \(10^{-3}\) makes the reconstructions
slightly inexact. The two eigenvalues become \(10^{-8}\) and \(10^{-7}\), still 40 times below the
third, and \(R^2\) drops only to 0.999. A large ridge, \(10^{-1}\), pulls the weights towards plain
averages, which bend the coordinates near the edges of the sheet (\(R^2\) 0.98 and 0.95). The
constraints hold to \(10^{-14}\) in every case.

>> The Swiss roll

The Swiss roll (`dm::datasets::swiss_roll`) is the spiral of the last section extruded to
height 21: \((t\cos t,\ h,\ t\sin t)\) with \(t\) uniform on \([1.5\pi,4.5\pi]\) and \(h\) uniform on
\([0,21]\). Unrolled, it is a flat \(89\times21\) rectangle with coordinates (arc length \(s\), height
\(h\)), and the generator returns both. We embed 1000 points into two dimensions. Each method is
scored three ways. The \(R^2\) of \(s\) and of \(h\), each regressed on \((Y_1,Y_2)\), is 1 for an
unrolling up to an affine map. Trustworthiness and continuity \(T(10)\) and \(C(10)\)
(`stats::trustworthiness`, Venna and Kaski 2001) range over \([0,1]\). \(T\) penalises points that
are neighbours in the embedding but not in the data (folds), and \(C\) penalises the reverse
(tears).

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/lle_swiss_roll.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const auto roll = dm::datasets::swiss_roll(1000, 0.0, 1);
  const std::vector<std::string> xyz{"x", "y", "z"};
  const dm::stats::LLE lle(roll, xyz, {.n_components = 2, .n_neighbors = 12});
  const dm::stats::PCA pca(roll, xyz, {.center = true, .scale = false});
  std::print("{}\n", lle.summary());

  const auto& s = roll.numeric_column("arc_length").values();
  const auto by_s = dm::graphics::value_colors(s);
  const auto by_h = dm::graphics::value_colors(roll.numeric_column("height").values());
  auto panel = [](const std::vector<double>& a, const std::vector<double>& b, const std::vector<dm::RGB>& col,
                  const std::string& title, const std::string& xl, const std::string& yl) {
    auto p = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
    p.points_colored(a, b, col, 3.0).title(title).x_label(xl).y_label(yl);
    return p;
  };
  auto fig = dm::graphics::RLayout::create(2, 2);
  fig.add(panel(roll.numeric_column("x").values(), roll.numeric_column("z").values(), by_s,
                "The roll end on, coloured by s", "x", "z"))
      .add(panel(pca.component_scores(0), pca.component_scores(1), by_s, "PCA, coloured by s", "PC1", "PC2"))
      .add(panel(lle.dimension(0), lle.dimension(1), by_s, "LLE (k = 12), coloured by s", "Y1", "Y2"))
      .add(panel(lle.dimension(0), lle.dimension(1), by_h, "LLE (k = 12), coloured by height", "Y1", "Y2"))
      .size(1100, 900)
      .fit_panels();
  fig.save_svg("build/lle_swiss_roll.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
Locally Linear Embedding
Features: x, y, z
Distance metric: euclidean
n_neighbors: 12, regularization: 0.001
Number of obs: 1000, dimensions: 2
Neighbour-graph components: 1
Embedding eigenvalues (costs):
  Dim1: 1.8053e-08
  Dim2: 2.5330e-07

\image(figures/lle_swiss_roll.svg)
// result_end

\caption(The Swiss roll (1000 points) and two embeddings, coloured by arc length \(s\) (viridis, purple at the inner end) or by height. Top left: the roll seen along its axis. Top right: the first two principal components. Bottom: LLE with \(k=12\) and the default ridge, coloured by \(s\) (left) and by height (right).)
\alttext(Top left: a spiral of points coloured purple at the centre to yellow at the outer end. Top right: PCA shows the spiral flattened with layers overlapping: purple inner points sit inside a ring of blue and green points, so far-apart parts of the roll share the same place. Bottom left: LLE spreads the points into a wedge, purple at the wide left end, green to yellow at the narrow right end, so colour changes smoothly from left to right. Bottom right: the same wedge coloured by height: purple at the top and yellow at the bottom on the left half, but on the right half the colours are compressed into a thin bent band.)

LLE turns the roll into a single sheet: colour by \(s\) changes monotonically from left to right
and no layers overlap, while PCA stacks the inner turn inside the outer one. The sheet is
distorted into a wedge, though. The narrow end squeezes the height into a thin band, the
classical look of LLE on this example (scikit-learn's gallery shows the same shape). The tables
below vary \(k\) and \(r\).

```{cpp, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

using DF = dm::dstruct::DataFrame;
using Matrix = dm::linalg::DenseMatrix<double>;

int main() {
  const auto roll = dm::datasets::swiss_roll(1000, 0.0, 1);
  const std::vector<std::string> xyz{"x", "y", "z"};
  const auto& t = roll.numeric_column("t").values();

  DF rows;
  using Cell = std::optional<double>;
  auto add = [&](const std::string& method, Cell k, Cell bridges, const Matrix& Y) {
    DF fit = roll;
    fit.add_column("Y1", Y.col(0));
    fit.add_column("Y2", Y.col(1));
    DF row;
    row.add_column("method", std::vector<std::string>{method});
    row.add_column("k", std::vector<Cell>{k});
    row.add_column("bridging edges", std::vector<Cell>{bridges});
    row.add_column("R2 of s", std::vector<double>{dm::stats::LM(fit, "arc_length ~ Y1 + Y2").r_squared()});
    row.add_column("R2 of h", std::vector<double>{dm::stats::LM(fit, "height ~ Y1 + Y2").r_squared()});
    row.add_column("T(10)", std::vector<double>{dm::stats::trustworthiness(roll, xyz, Y, 10)});
    row.add_column("C(10)", std::vector<double>{dm::stats::continuity(roll, xyz, Y, 10)});
    rows = rows.nrows() == 0 ? row : rows.bind_rows(row);
  };
  // A bridging edge joins neighbours on different layers of the roll (|t_i - t_j| > 3; layers are 2 pi apart).
  auto bridges = [&](const std::vector<std::vector<std::size_t>>& neighbors) {
    double count = 0;
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < neighbors.size(); ++i)
      for (const auto j : neighbors[i]) count += std::abs(t[i] - t[j]) > 3.0;
    return count;
  };
  for (const std::size_t k : {5, 6, 8, 10, 12, 15, 20, 30}) {
    const dm::stats::LLE lle(roll, xyz, {.n_components = 2, .n_neighbors = k});
    add("LLE", static_cast<double>(k), bridges(lle.neighbors()), lle.embedding());
  }
  for (const std::size_t k : {6, 10}) {
    const dm::stats::LLE same_graph(roll, xyz, {.n_neighbors = k});
    const dm::stats::Isomap iso(roll, xyz, {.n_components = 2, .n_neighbors = k});
    add("Isomap", static_cast<double>(k), bridges(same_graph.neighbors()), iso.embedding());
  }
  const dm::stats::PCA pca(roll, xyz, {.center = true, .scale = false});
  add("PCA", std::nullopt, std::nullopt, Matrix::from_columns({pca.component_scores(0), pca.component_scores(1)}));
  DF::MarkdownOptions md;
  md.na = "";
  std::print("{}\n", rows.to_md(md));
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| method | k | bridging edges | R2 of s | R2 of h | T(10) | C(10) |
| :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| LLE | 5 | 0 | 0.426 | 0.008 | 0.898 | 0.974 |
| LLE | 6 | 0 | 0.994 | 0.241 | 0.980 | 0.992 |
| LLE | 8 | 1 | 0.991 | 0.765 | 0.995 | 0.995 |
| LLE | 10 | 4 | 0.981 | 0.729 | 0.996 | 0.996 |
| LLE | 12 | 8 | 0.987 | 0.695 | 0.996 | 0.996 |
| LLE | 15 | 14 | 0.994 | 0.689 | 0.996 | 0.996 |
| LLE | 20 | 46 | 0.438 | 0.572 | 0.878 | 0.982 |
| LLE | 30 | 251 | 0.001 | 0.962 | 0.862 | 0.988 |
| Isomap | 6 | 0 | 1.000 | 0.973 | 0.999 | 0.999 |
| Isomap | 10 | 4 | 0.842 | 0.030 | 0.986 | 0.995 |
| PCA |  |  | 0.084 | 0.246 | 0.944 | 0.994 |
// result_end

\caption(Two-dimensional embeddings of the Swiss roll for a range of neighbourhood sizes \(k\) (LLE with \(r=10^{-3}\)), Isomap on the same neighbour graphs, and PCA. Bridging edges: neighbour pairs on different layers of the roll. \(R^2\): the true coordinate regressed on the two embedding coordinates. \(T\), \(C\): trustworthiness and continuity with 10 neighbours.)

There is a band of good \(k\), from 6 to 15, where the arc length is recovered with
\(R^2\ge0.98\) and trustworthiness is about 0.996. Below it, \(k=5\) gives too few neighbours to
span the two local directions robustly. Above it, the number of bridging edges grows, from 8 at
\(k=12\) to 46 at \(k=20\) and 251 at \(k=30\), and the unrolling collapses: a bridge tells LLE that
two layers are the same place. A few bridges are tolerated, because each point has several
other neighbours on its own layer. Isomap is the contrast. It embeds the shortest-path distances
of the neighbour graph, so on a clean graph (\(k=6\), no bridges) it unrolls the sheet almost
perfectly, recovering both \(s\) and \(h\) (\(R^2\) 1.000 and 0.973), far better than LLE. But
one bridge shortcuts every path that uses it: at \(k=10\) four bridges lower its \(R^2\) of \(s\)
to 0.84 and lose the height. LLE never recovers the height well, which the next two sections
explain.

The regularisation decides which coordinates are cheap, as the spiral showed. The following
table varies \(r\) at \(k=12\). It gives the costs of the true coordinates \(s\), \(h\) and of the raw
coordinates \(x\), \(z\) (each centred and scaled to unit length), and how much of the embedding is
a linear function of the raw coordinates.

```{cpp, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

using DF = dm::dstruct::DataFrame;

int main() {
  const auto roll = dm::datasets::swiss_roll(1000, 0.0, 1);
  std::vector<double> regs{1e-9, 1e-6, 1e-4, 1e-3, 1e-2, 1e-1, 1.0}, l1, cs, ch, cx, cz, lin, r2s, r2h, trust;
  for (const double r : regs) {
    const dm::stats::LLE lle(roll, {"x", "y", "z"}, {.n_components = 2, .n_neighbors = 12, .regularization = r});
    l1.push_back(lle.eigenvalues()[998]);
    cs.push_back(lle.cost(roll.numeric_column("arc_length").values()));
    ch.push_back(lle.cost(roll.numeric_column("height").values()));
    cx.push_back(lle.cost(roll.numeric_column("x").values()));
    cz.push_back(lle.cost(roll.numeric_column("z").values()));
    DF fit = roll;
    fit.add_column("Y1", lle.dimension(0));
    fit.add_column("Y2", lle.dimension(1));
    lin.push_back(0.5 * (dm::stats::LM(fit, "Y1 ~ x + y + z").r_squared() + dm::stats::LM(fit, "Y2 ~ x + y + z").r_squared()));
    r2s.push_back(dm::stats::LM(fit, "arc_length ~ Y1 + Y2").r_squared());
    r2h.push_back(dm::stats::LM(fit, "height ~ Y1 + Y2").r_squared());
    trust.push_back(dm::stats::trustworthiness(roll, {"x", "y", "z"}, lle.embedding(), 10));
  }
  DF table;
  table.add_column("r", regs);
  table.add_column("lambda_1", l1);
  table.add_column("cost of s", cs);
  table.add_column("cost of h", ch);
  table.add_column("cost of x", cx);
  table.add_column("cost of z", cz);
  table.add_column("linear in x,y,z", lin);
  table.add_column("R2 of s", r2s);
  table.add_column("R2 of h", r2h);
  table.add_column("T(10)", trust);
  DF::MarkdownOptions md;
  md.digits = 1;
  md.column_digits = {{"r", 0}, {"linear in x,y,z", 3}, {"R2 of s", 3}, {"R2 of h", 3}, {"T(10)", 3}};
  md.scientific = {"r", "lambda_1", "cost of s", "cost of h", "cost of x", "cost of z"};
  std::print("{}\n", table.to_md(md));
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| r | lambda_1 | cost of s | cost of h | cost of x | cost of z | linear in x,y,z | R2 of s | R2 of h | T(10) |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 1e-09 | -6.3e-15 | 1.1e-05 | 1.2e-18 | 7.6e-17 | 8.0e-17 | 1.000 | 0.069 | 0.670 | 0.874 |
| 1e-06 | 8.7e-15 | 1.1e-05 | 1.2e-12 | 7.5e-11 | 7.9e-11 | 0.973 | 0.243 | 0.997 | 0.828 |
| 1e-04 | 3.2e-11 | 1.1e-05 | 1.1e-08 | 5.6e-07 | 5.4e-07 | 0.453 | 0.937 | 0.769 | 0.969 |
| 1e-03 | 1.8e-08 | 1.2e-05 | 8.2e-07 | 1.9e-05 | 1.8e-05 | 0.421 | 0.987 | 0.695 | 0.996 |
| 1e-02 | 7.9e-07 | 3.4e-05 | 4.6e-05 | 1.3e-04 | 1.2e-04 | 0.311 | 0.979 | 0.033 | 0.988 |
| 1e-01 | 2.9e-06 | 4.2e-04 | 1.4e-03 | 5.2e-04 | 3.6e-04 | 0.406 | 0.957 | 0.006 | 0.940 |
| 1e+00 | 1.3e-06 | 1.3e-03 | 6.9e-03 | 2.1e-03 | 1.4e-03 | 0.416 | 0.964 | 0.004 | 0.938 |
// result_end

\caption(LLE of the Swiss roll with \(k=12\) for ridges from \(10^{-9}\) to 1: the smallest non-trivial eigenvalue; the costs \(v^\top Mv\) of the unit-length centred arc length, height and raw coordinates \(x\), \(z\); the mean \(R^2\) of the two embedding coordinates regressed on \((x,y,z)\); the \(R^2\) of \(s\) and \(h\) on the embedding; and trustworthiness.)

The theory predicts the first row exactly. With \(r=10^{-9}\), twelve weights reconstruct each
point of \(\mathbb R^3\) exactly, the raw coordinates cost about \(10^{-16}\), and the embedding *is* a
linear projection of the data (\(R^2\) of 1.000 on \((x,y,z)\)). It is no better than PCA
(\(R^2\) of \(s\) 0.07). The height \(h=y\) is itself a raw coordinate, which is why \(R^2\) of \(h\)
is high in the first two rows. As \(r\) grows from \(10^{-6}\) to \(10^{-4}\), the raw coordinates'
cost rises like \(r^2\), while the cost of \(s\) barely moves. Between \(10^{-4}\) and \(10^{-3}\)
they cross, and that is where the unrolling appears. At \(r=10^{-3}\), scikit-learn's default,
\(x\) and \(z\) cost \(1.9\times10^{-5}\) against \(1.2\times10^{-5}\) for \(s\). The linear part of the
embedding falls to about 0.4, which is the part the height, a raw coordinate, explains. For
\(r\ge10^{-2}\) the weights approach plain averages, all costs grow together, and the height is lost
(\(R^2\le0.03\)) though \(s\) survives. Trustworthiness peaks at the default. The window in which
both the arc length and some of the height come through is about one decade wide.

>> Which eigenvectors come first

LLE returns the cheapest coordinates, which need not be the ones we hope for. The bottom
eigenvectors of \(M\) behave like the smooth vibration modes of the sheet (Belkin and Niyogi 2003
relate \(M\) to the square of the manifold's Laplacian). On an \(L_1\times L_2\) rectangle, the low
modes are \(\cos(m\pi s/L_1)\cos(n\pi h/L_2)\), ordered by \((m/L_1)^2+(n/L_2)^2\). The Swiss roll's sheet
is \(89\times21\), about 4.25 times longer than tall, so on this ordering the first few harmonics
along \(s\), \(\cos(m\pi s/L_1)\) for \(m\le4\), are cheaper than the first mode across, \(\cos(\pi h/L_2)\).
The picture is only a heuristic for LLE, but it predicts the competition: the second coordinate
can be \(h\) or \(\cos(2\pi s/L_1)\), a function of \(s\) that carries no new information. To see what LLE actually finds, we compute five coordinates and
regress each on \(s\), on the harmonics \(\cos(m\pi u)\) with \(u=(s-s_{\min})/(s_{\max}-s_{\min})\), and on
\(h\). Then we stretch the roll's height to make the sheet less elongated.

```{cpp, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

using DF = dm::dstruct::DataFrame;

int main() {
  auto roll = dm::datasets::swiss_roll(1000, 0.0, 1);
  const auto s = roll.numeric_column("arc_length").values();
  const auto [s_min, s_max] = std::ranges::minmax(s);
  std::vector<double> c1, c2, c3;
  for (const double si : s) {
    const double u = std::numbers::pi * (si - s_min) / (s_max - s_min);
    c1.push_back(std::cos(u)), c2.push_back(std::cos(2 * u)), c3.push_back(std::cos(3 * u));
  }
  roll.add_column("cos1", c1);
  roll.add_column("cos2", c2);
  roll.add_column("cos3", c3);

  const dm::stats::LLE lle(roll, {"x", "y", "z"}, {.n_components = 5, .n_neighbors = 12});
  const std::vector<std::string> targets{"arc_length", "cos1", "cos2", "cos3", "height"};
  std::vector<double> eig;
  std::vector<std::vector<double>> r2(targets.size());
  for (std::size_t c = 0; c < 5; ++c) {
    eig.push_back(lle.eigenvalues()[998 - c]);
    DF fit = roll;
    fit.add_column("Y", lle.dimension(c));
    for (std::size_t j = 0; j < targets.size(); ++j) r2[j].push_back(dm::stats::LM(fit, targets[j] + " ~ Y").r_squared());
  }
  DF table;
  table.add_column("coordinate", dm::seql(1.0, 5.0, 5).values());
  table.add_column("eigenvalue", eig);
  for (std::size_t j = 0; j < targets.size(); ++j) table.add_column("R2 " + targets[j], r2[j]);
  DF::MarkdownOptions md;
  md.digits = 2;
  md.column_digits = {{"eigenvalue", 1}};
  md.scientific = {"eigenvalue"};
  std::print("{}\n\n", table.to_md(md));

  // Stretch the height so the sheet is less elongated.
  std::vector<double> factors{1.0, 1.5, 2.0, 3.0, 4.0}, ratio, rs, rh;
  for (const double f : factors) {
    DF tall = roll;
    tall.add_column("y_tall", (f * dm::Vector<>(roll.numeric_column("y").values())).values());
    const dm::stats::LLE stretched(tall, {"x", "y_tall", "z"}, {.n_components = 2, .n_neighbors = 8});
    tall.add_column("Y1", stretched.dimension(0));
    tall.add_column("Y2", stretched.dimension(1));
    ratio.push_back((s_max - s_min) / (21.0 * f));
    rs.push_back(dm::stats::LM(tall, "arc_length ~ Y1 + Y2").r_squared());
    rh.push_back(dm::stats::LM(tall, "height ~ Y1 + Y2").r_squared());
  }
  DF stretch;
  stretch.add_column("height factor", factors);
  stretch.add_column("length / height", ratio);
  stretch.add_column("R2 of s", rs);
  stretch.add_column("R2 of h", rh);
  std::print("{}\n", stretch.to_md());
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| coordinate | eigenvalue | R2 arc_length | R2 cos1 | R2 cos2 | R2 cos3 | R2 height |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 1 | 1.8e-08 | 0.98 | 0.96 | 0.13 | 0.05 | 0.01 |
| 2 | 2.5e-07 | 0.01 | 0.01 | 0.08 | 0.03 | 0.68 |
| 3 | 3.4e-07 | 0.00 | 0.01 | 0.63 | 0.14 | 0.16 |
| 4 | 9.4e-07 | 0.01 | 0.01 | 0.01 | 0.03 | 0.14 |
| 5 | 2.6e-06 | 0.00 | 0.00 | 0.13 | 0.43 | 0.00 |


| height factor | length / height | R2 of s | R2 of h |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 1.0 | 4.250 | 0.991 | 0.765 |
| 1.5 | 2.833 | 0.785 | 0.790 |
| 2.0 | 2.125 | 0.935 | 0.913 |
| 3.0 | 1.417 | 0.070 | 0.955 |
| 4.0 | 1.062 | 0.003 | 0.992 |
// result_end

\caption(Top: the first five LLE coordinates of the Swiss roll (\(k=12\)), each regressed on the arc length, on three cosine harmonics of it and on the height. Bottom: two-dimensional LLE (\(k=8\)) of the roll with its height multiplied by a factor, so that the unrolled sheet is less elongated.)

The first coordinate is the arc length (\(R^2=0.98\)), or equally its first harmonic. Coordinates
2 to 4 have eigenvalues within a factor of 4 of each other and split the height between them
(\(R^2\) 0.68, 0.16 and 0.14), mixed with the second harmonic of \(s\) (0.63 in coordinate 3).
Coordinate 5 is mostly the third harmonic. The height arrives earlier than the rectangle
ordering suggests, but not as one clean coordinate, so its \(R^2\) on the first two coordinates
stays near 0.7. Stretching the height changes the balance: at twice the height (length/height
2.1) both coordinates are recovered with \(R^2>0.9\). The response is not monotone, though. At
1.5 times the height both are worse, and from 3 times on the arc length drops out of the first
two coordinates altogether, although the rectangle ordering would still put it first. LLE has
no notion of which smooth function is a "coordinate". Asking for more dimensions than needed
and choosing among them is often necessary.

> A dataset example

>> Iris: a neighbour graph in two pieces

Fisher's iris data (`dm::datasets::iris()`) has four measurements of 150 flowers from three
species. The species are not used in the fit. Setosa is far from the other two species in petal
size, so for small \(k\) no setosa flower has a neighbour of another species, nor the reverse,
and the neighbour graph has two components.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/lle_iris.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

using DF = dm::dstruct::DataFrame;

int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const auto iris = dm::datasets::iris();
  const std::vector<std::string> features{"Sepal.Length", "Sepal.Width", "Petal.Length", "Petal.Width"};
  const auto& species = iris.string_column("Species").values();

  std::vector<double> ks{5, 10, 20, 24, 25, 30, 50}, comps, trust;
  for (const double k : ks) {
    const dm::stats::LLE lle(iris, features, {.n_neighbors = static_cast<std::size_t>(k)});
    comps.push_back(static_cast<double>(lle.connected_components()));
    trust.push_back(dm::stats::trustworthiness(iris, features, lle.embedding(), 10));
  }
  DF sweep;
  sweep.add_column("k", ks);
  sweep.add_column("components", comps);
  sweep.add_column("T(10)", trust);
  std::print("{}\n\n", sweep.to_md());

  const dm::stats::LLE split(iris, features, {.n_neighbors = 10});
  DF support = iris;
  support.add_column("Y1", split.dimension(0));
  support.add_column("Y2", split.dimension(1));
  const auto ranges = support | dm::group_by("Species") |
                      dm::summarise(dm::agg::min("Y1", "min Y1"), dm::agg::max("Y1", "max Y1"),
                                    dm::agg::min("Y2", "min Y2"), dm::agg::max("Y2", "max Y2"));
  DF::MarkdownOptions md;
  md.digits = 1;
  md.scientific = {"min Y1", "max Y1", "min Y2", "max Y2"};
  std::print("{}\n", ranges.to_md(md));

  const dm::stats::PCA pca(iris, features, {.center = true, .scale = false});
  const auto pcs = dm::linalg::DenseMatrix<double>::from_columns({pca.component_scores(0), pca.component_scores(1)});
  std::print("PCA: T(10) = {:.3f}.\n", dm::stats::trustworthiness(iris, features, pcs, 10));

  const dm::stats::LLE joined(iris, features, {.n_neighbors = 30});
  auto fig = dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1, 2);
  fig.add(split.plot_embedding(species).title("k = 10: two components").y_limits(-0.35, 0.5))
      .add(joined.plot_embedding(species).title("k = 30: one component").y_limits(-0.35, 0.5))
      .size(1100, 480)
      .fit_panels();
  fig.save_svg("build/lle_iris.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| k | components | T(10) |
| ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 5 | 2 | 0.912 |
| 10 | 2 | 0.913 |
| 20 | 2 | 0.917 |
| 24 | 2 | 0.913 |
| 25 | 1 | 0.944 |
| 30 | 1 | 0.980 |
| 50 | 1 | 0.974 |


| Species | min Y1 | max Y1 | min Y2 | max Y2 |
| :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| setosa | 0.0e+00 | 0.0e+00 | -2.6e-01 | 3.5e-01 |
| versicolor | -1.8e-01 | 1.8e-02 | 0.0e+00 | 0.0e+00 |
| virginica | -7.9e-02 | 2.7e-01 | 0.0e+00 | 0.0e+00 |

PCA: T(10) = 0.983.
\image(figures/lle_iris.svg)
// result_end

\caption(LLE of the four iris measurements, coloured by species (not used in the fit). Left: \(k=10\), where the neighbour graph has two components. Right: \(k=30\), where it is connected. Tables: components and trustworthiness against \(k\); the range of each coordinate within each species at \(k=10\).)
\alttext(Left: a cross. The 50 setosa points lie on the vertical axis from about minus 0.26 to 0.35 with Dim1 exactly 0; the versicolor and virginica points lie on the horizontal axis from about minus 0.18 to 0.27 with Dim2 exactly 0, versicolor mostly left of virginica. Right: setosa forms a vertical column at Dim1 about minus 0.1; versicolor and virginica form one rising band to the right, versicolor at the left end and virginica at the right.)

The graph stays split up to \(k=24\) and joins at \(k=25\). In the split case each coordinate lives
on one component, as the theory says. The first coordinate is exactly zero on every setosa
flower and orders versicolor against virginica. The second is zero on versicolor and virginica
and spreads setosa out. The picture is a cross whose arms are unrelated: the distance between a
setosa point and a versicolor point means nothing. With \(k=30\) the graph is connected, the two
groups are placed relative to each other, and trustworthiness rises from 0.913 to 0.980. Iris
is nearly linear, though: PCA's two components are as trustworthy (0.983). LLE adds nothing
here once the graph is connected. Its useful lesson is the diagnostic: check
`connected_components()` before interpreting the axes.

>> Handwritten digits

The 8×8 handwritten digits (`dm::datasets::digits()`) are 64-dimensional, and images of one digit
vary in a few ways: slant, thickness, size. We embed the 1083 images of the digits 0 to 5.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/lle_digits.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

using DF = dm::dstruct::DataFrame;

int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const auto digits = dm::datasets::digits().filter([](const auto& row) { return row.get_double("digit") < 6; });
  std::vector<std::string> pixels, labels;
  for (int i = 0; i < 64; ++i) pixels.push_back(std::format("pixel_{}", i));
  for (const double d : digits.numeric_column("digit").values()) labels.push_back(std::format("{}", d));

  const dm::stats::PCA pca(digits, pixels, {.center = true, .scale = false});
  const auto pcs = dm::linalg::DenseMatrix<double>::from_columns({pca.component_scores(0), pca.component_scores(1)});
  std::vector<std::string> method{"PCA"};
  std::vector<double> trust{dm::stats::trustworthiness(digits, pixels, pcs, 10)};
  std::vector<double> cont{dm::stats::continuity(digits, pixels, pcs, 10)};
  for (const std::size_t k : {10, 30}) {
    const dm::stats::LLE lle(digits, pixels, {.n_neighbors = k});
    method.push_back(std::format("LLE, k = {} ({} component)", k, lle.connected_components()));
    trust.push_back(dm::stats::trustworthiness(digits, pixels, lle.embedding(), 10));
    cont.push_back(dm::stats::continuity(digits, pixels, lle.embedding(), 10));
  }
  DF table;
  table.add_column("method", method);
  table.add_column("T(10)", trust);
  table.add_column("C(10)", cont);
  std::print("{} images of the digits 0 to 5.\n\n{}\n", digits.nrows(), table.to_md());

  const dm::stats::LLE lle(digits, pixels, {.n_neighbors = 10});
  auto fig = dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1, 2);
  fig.add(pca.plot_scores(labels).title("PCA").y_limits(-35.0, 75.0))
      .add(lle.plot_embedding(labels).title("LLE, k = 10").y_limits(-0.05, 0.135))
      .size(1100, 480)
      .fit_panels();
  fig.save_svg("build/lle_digits.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
1083 images of the digits 0 to 5.

| method | T(10) | C(10) |
| :--- | ---: | ---: |
| PCA | 0.868 | 0.958 |
| LLE, k = 10 (1 component) | 0.943 | 0.974 |
| LLE, k = 30 (1 component) | 0.785 | 0.881 |

\image(figures/lle_digits.svg)
// result_end

\caption(The 1083 images of the digits 0 to 5 embedded in two dimensions, coloured by digit (not used in the fit). Left: the first two principal components. Right: LLE with \(k=10\). Table: trustworthiness and continuity with 10 neighbours.)
\alttext(Left: PCA shows six overlapping clouds: 0 at the bottom, 4 at the left, 1 at the top, 2 at the right, 3 at the lower right, and 5 in the middle overlapping 1, 2 and 3. Right: LLE shows compact, mostly separate groups: 4 as a short vertical strip at the bottom left, 1 as a strip at the left plus a small strip near the top, 5 as a diagonal streak in the lower middle, 3 as a cluster in the centre right, 2 spread across the top centre, and 0 collapsed into a tight dot at the far right.)

LLE with \(k=10\) separates the six digits into compact groups, and its trustworthiness (0.943)
is well above PCA's (0.868): its neighbours in the plane are mostly neighbours in pixel space.
Each group is squeezed into a short curve or a dot (all the zeros land on nearly one point). LLE's unit-variance constraint lets the
cheapest solution put whole clusters almost on top of each other along one direction, a known
weakness that modified variants (Hessian LLE, LTSA, modified LLE) address. With \(k=30\)
neighbourhoods span several digits, and trustworthiness drops below PCA's.

> Conclusion

LLE reduces nonlinear dimensionality reduction to linear algebra: a small constrained least
squares problem per point, then the bottom eigenvectors of one sparse matrix. Each step was
checked here against a closed form: the minimum-norm weights \((5,5,6,3)/19\), the line's
embedding \((3-i)/\sqrt{28}\), and the exact recovery of a flat sheet. The theory also explains
the failures. With \(k\) larger than the input dimension and too small a ridge, the raw
coordinates become free and LLE returns a linear projection, so the ridge is essential and has a
window of about one decade. Too large a \(k\) bridges separate parts of the manifold. A
disconnected neighbour graph embeds its components on unrelated axes. On a long thin sheet, the
short direction competes with harmonics of the long one, so the coordinate we want may not be
among the first two. When these are respected, LLE unrolls the Swiss roll's long direction
(\(R^2=0.99\)), where PCA gets 0.08, and separates handwritten digits more faithfully than PCA. It
needs no distances along the manifold. That makes it far less sensitive than Isomap to a few
bridging edges, although Isomap on a clean neighbour graph unrolls the roll better. It does need care with \(k\) and \(r\), and its output is a distorted, unit-variance
picture, not a map that preserves distances.

> References

- S. T. Roweis and L. K. Saul (2000). "Nonlinear dimensionality reduction by locally linear embedding". *Science* 290(5500), 2323–2326. doi:10.1126/science.290.5500.2323
- L. K. Saul and S. T. Roweis (2003). "Think globally, fit locally: unsupervised learning of low dimensional manifolds". *Journal of Machine Learning Research* 4, 119–155.
- M. Belkin and P. Niyogi (2003). "Laplacian eigenmaps for dimensionality reduction and data representation". *Neural Computation* 15(6), 1373–1396. doi:10.1162/089976603321780317
- J. Venna and S. Kaski (2001). "Neighborhood preservation in nonlinear projection methods: an experimental study". *ICANN 2001*, LNCS 2130, 485–491. doi:10.1007/3-540-44668-0_68
- J. B. Tenenbaum, V. de Silva and J. C. Langford (2000). "A global geometric framework for nonlinear dimensionality reduction". *Science* 290(5500), 2319–2323. doi:10.1126/science.290.5500.2319
- scikit-learn, `LocallyLinearEmbedding`: https://scikit-learn.org/stable/modules/generated/sklearn.manifold.LocallyLinearEmbedding.html
